  "query_preview": "/Friday through Monday/... \nLittle will change with the weather pattern for the rest of this \nweek, and likely well into next week as the high pressure ridge is \nexpected to remain centered over the Desert Southwest. The center of \nthe ridge is forecast to somewhat shift farther to the north by \nFriday which could help to bring better rain chances, but guidance \nshows the ridge also strengthening which will likely reinforce the \nsubsidence over much of our area. As of now, Friday is expected to \nfollow the same pattern as today and Wednesday with mostly eastern \nArizona high terrain storms and very conditional 5-15% PoPs into the \nsouth-central Arizona lower deserts. As stated in the short-term \ndiscussion, even though it will be a day-to-day low chance \nenvironment for the lower deserts, it would not be surprising to \nhave a day where the elevated instability is managed to be tapped \ninto causing some scattered storms to fire. Hot and humid conditions \nwill continue to prevail with daytime highs likely nudging upward \nagain late week as the ridge restrengthens. The Extreme Heat Warning \ncurrently goes through Friday which is very reasonable, but it will \nbe possible to have a day in there where it fails to reach criteria \nover south-central Arizona if convection ends up being more \nwidespread one evening/overnight.\n\nFor the weekend into early next week, ensembles still favor the \nridge basically staying stationary with the center somewhere just to \nour north. Some weakening of the ridge is likely to occur by early \nnext week and depending on how much moisture will be available we \ncould be looking at a further uptick in monsoon activity. The GEFS \nis more optimistic with rain chances by early next week with \nactivity expanding into central and northern Arizona, but the EPS \nholds off until later next week. If convection does eventually \nbecome more widespread and impacts the lower deserts, temperatures \nare expected to dip closer to seasonal normals.",
